 "response": "Here’s a concise implementation that simply compares the string to its reverse after normalising the case:\n\n```python\ndef is_palindrome(s: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Return True if `s` reads the same forwards and backwards, ignoring case.\n    \n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    s : str\n        The string to test.\n    \n    Returns\n    -------\n    bool\n        True if the string is a palindrome (case‑insensitive), otherwise False.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Normalise to a single case (lowercase here) and compare with its reverse.\n    lowered = s.lower()\n    return lowered == lowered[::-1]\n```\n\n### Example usage\n\n```python\n>>> is_palindrome(\"Racecar\")\nTrue\n>>> is_palindrome(\"Hello\")\nFalse\n>>> is_palindrome(\"A\")\nTrue\n>>> is_palindrome(\"\")\nTrue\n```\n\nThis function treats the string as a whole; if you need to ignore spaces, punctuation, or other non‑alphanumeric characters, you’d first filter those out before performing the comparison. But as requested, it only ignores case.",
